  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Last round of showers and thunderstorms pushes\nthrough this afternoon and evening with the arrival of a cold \nfront. Dry and seasonable day expected Tuesday.\n\nExpect widespread showers with pockets of moderate to heavy rain \npossible through the late afternoon. A cold front approaches \nfrom the west with a leading line of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. Originally this posed a threat for some strong to\nsevere thunderstorms. We are still in a marginally unstable \nairmass with SPC mesoanalysis SBCAPE values around 500-1,000 \nJ/kg accompanied by rich moisture. Enough to support \nthunderstorms in the mix of that line. The risk for severe will \nbe on the lower side as we didn't have enough clearing to allow \nthe  to re- bound/destabilize enough. Given the deep moisture, \nbrief downpours are also possible within these cells. This line \nwill push through western MA this afternoon then clearing the \neast coast by 8 PM. Winds decrease and shift to the west with \nthe cold front accompanied by scattering and clearing clouds. \nDry conditions expected tonight. Can't rule out some patchy fog \nin the valleys and the Cape/Islands once they areas clear if \nthere is any residual moisture. \n\nQuiet day for Tuesday with a weak mid-level ridge building over the \nregion. This will support dry conditions and seasonable temperatures \nin the low to mid 80s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Summer heat and humidity slowly returns late Wed, \nthen peaks Thu/Fri and likely lingers into the weekend. Diurnal \nscattered thunderstorms each day, but many hours of dry weather too, \nhence, not a washout. \n\nSummer heat and humidity slowly returns later Wed and then peaks \nThu/Fri and likely continuing into the weekend, as ensembles are in \ngood agreement on 597+ dam 500 mb subtropical ridge/Bermuda high \nbuilding across the Western Atlantic. This will circulate a very \nwarm and humid airmass into SNE, with dew pts 70-75 Thu/Fri and \npossibly lingering into the weekend. With 925 mb temps 22C+ each \nafternoon, highs 85-90 are likely and combined with dew pts of 70-\n75, heat headlines are possible late this week and possibly into the \nweekend. \n\nSynoptic scale forcing is very weak Thu and Fri,thus, many hours of \ndry weather. However, short wave energy will eject northeast from \nremnant closed low currently over the southeast states into SNE. \nThen over the weekend, mean trough axis sets up from central Canada \ninto the Western Great Lakes, with broad cyclonic flow into New \nEngland. This combined with heat/humidity/instability will be \nsufficient for typical summer, scattered diurnal convection Thu/Fri \nand into the weekend. With PWATs +1.5 to +2.0 sigma, any storm will \nhave the potential for tropical/torrential downpours. Again, many \nhours of dry weather too during this time period. Thus, not a \nwashout.",
